WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:07.200
In Python, a for loop is designed to iterate over&nbsp;
a sequence, collection, or data structure, such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.480
as a list, tuple, or string. Unlike a while loop,&nbsp;
which runs as long as a certain condition is true,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:20.240
a for loop runs for a predetermined number of&nbsp;
iterations based on the length of the collection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:25.120
For our first example, we will use a for loop&nbsp;
to track a player moving through a series of 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:33.560
positions on a game grid. In this 222-for file,&nbsp;
we have, on line 1, a position variable set to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:41.600
the number of positions, 10. The for statement&nbsp;
starts on line 2. P is a variable we are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:49.280
using while inside the for loop, and the range&nbsp;
signifies the number of times the loop should run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:57.720
which in this case is 10. Line 3 is indented and&nbsp;
contains a print statement to tell the game player&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:06.400
what position has been reached. I will run this&nbsp;
code and we see something quite interesting in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:14.760
results. The loop ran 10 times, as expected, but&nbsp;
look at the numbers we see within these results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:22.160
The positions are 0 through 9, not 1 through&nbsp;
10. As is the case with getting items in lists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:30.120
for loops are also zero-based by default. However,&nbsp;
with a slight change to the range, we can get the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:37.960
numbers 1 through 10 as desired in this code. On&nbsp;
line 2, I will add a 1 inside the parentheses and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:46.120
a comma to indicate that I want this range&nbsp;
to start at 1, not 0. Then, to get the loop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:53.840
to run to position 10, I will add +1 to the end of&nbsp;
positions. These changes will shift the range from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:02:04.840
0 to 9 to 1 to 10. I will rerun this code, and&nbsp;
we see the changes as expected. There is one more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:10.800
argument we can add to the range of a for loop: a&nbsp;
step which tells the loop to run in gaps based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:18.840
the step number indicated. For example, if I add&nbsp;
a comma and a 2 after the positions+1 part of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:28.720
range and then rerun the code, the loop starts&nbsp;
at 1 and then steps 2 numbers forward with each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:34.640
iteration of the loop. In a quiz situation,&nbsp;
this type of loop can work if one wants to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:41.600
for example, ask every other question from a pool&nbsp;
of questions. Knowing the for loop is important as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.120
one needs to know when a block of code needs&nbsp;
to be run a set number of times, as not all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:53.160
loops are conditional. To see the other type&nbsp;
of for example, which iterates through a list,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:03:01.680
let’s look at this 222-for-each file, which starts&nbsp;
with, on line 1, a tuple of coins a player has in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:12.680
a game. Then, on line 2, we see a for loop with a&nbsp;
new variable, coin, that is used to retrieve the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:21.880
coins we see on line 1. This word can be any word&nbsp;
or letter, but as a good practice, people tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:28.360
use the singular version of a word in a for loop&nbsp;
to loop through a plural version of the variable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:34.560
that stores a list type. The code on line 3 is&nbsp;
indented as part of the loop and contains a print&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:42.040
statement that concatenates text around the coin&nbsp;
variable. We have three coins, so this loop should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:51.640
run three times, and when I run this code, that is&nbsp;
exactly what happens. Again, for loops can be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:59.680
to run a block of code a set number of times or&nbsp;
iterate through a list such as this list of coins.

